\chapter{Introduction}

Chorus is a library for GPU programming from Common Lisp. It targets NVIDIA
GPUs through CUDA, Apple silicon, and AMD GPUs. It is a fork of
cl-cuda by Masayuki Takagi \cite{takagi-cl-cuda}. The implementation in this
tree is the CUDA backend. The chapters after this introduction describe that
backend.

The kernel language expresses kernel functions, kernel macros, and kernel
symbol macros as S-expressions. On the CUDA backend, a kernel is compiled to
CUDA C, then to PTX by \texttt{nvcc}, then loaded through the CUDA driver API.
The kernel manager does this the first time the kernel is launched. A launch
looks like an ordinary function call. It takes \texttt{:grid-dim} and
\texttt{:block-dim}, and on the CUDA backend it runs inside a CUDA context.

Memory blocks pair a host allocation with a device allocation.
\texttt{sync-memory-block} copies between them. The same package also exposes
the host pointer and the device pointer separately.

\section*{Targets}

\begin{itemize}
  \item NVIDIA GPUs, through CUDA.
  \item Apple silicon.
  \item AMD GPUs.
\end{itemize}

The CUDA backend is verified on Windows and Linux with a current CUDA
toolkit. An RTX 50-series GPU (Blackwell, compute capability 12.0) needs CUDA
Toolkit 12.8 or later. CUDA 13.x is the toolkit this backend is written for.
With CUDA 13, \texttt{nvcc} can target Turing and newer (\texttt{sm\_75} and
above).

The repository is at \url{https://github.com/cgore/chorus/}.

\section*{Requirements for the CUDA backend}

\begin{itemize}
  \item An NVIDIA CUDA-capable GPU.
  \item The CUDA driver (\texttt{nvcuda.dll} on Windows, \texttt{libcuda.so.1}
        on Linux).
  \item The CUDA Toolkit, so that \texttt{nvcc} can compile kernels to PTX.
  \item On Windows, Visual Studio with the C++ workload, so that \texttt{nvcc}
        has a host compiler.
\end{itemize}

Kernel files are written to the operating system's temporary directory unless
\texttt{*tmp-path*} is set. \texttt{nvcc} is found on \texttt{PATH}, through
\texttt{CUDA\_PATH} or \texttt{CUDA\_HOME}, or in the usual toolkit locations.

\section*{Loading the library}

Once the checkout is visible to ASDF:

\begin{lstlisting}
(ql:quickload :chorus)
\end{lstlisting}

The test suite runs when \texttt{chorus-test} is loaded:

\begin{lstlisting}
(asdf:test-system :chorus)
\end{lstlisting}

From a shell, in the checkout:

\begin{lstlisting}
sbcl --load ~/quicklisp/setup.lisp --load test/run.lisp
\end{lstlisting}

\section*{A kernel}

The following is the CUDA backend's form of \texttt{examples/vector-add.lisp}.

\begin{lstlisting}
(defkernel vec-add-kernel (void ((a float*) (b float*) (c float*) (n int)))
  (let ((i (+ (* block-dim-x block-idx-x) thread-idx-x)))
    (if (< i n)
        (set (aref c i)
             (+ (aref a i) (aref b i))))))

(defun main ()
  (let* ((dev-id 0)
         (n 1024)
         (threads-per-block 256)
         (blocks-per-grid (/ n threads-per-block)))
    (with-cuda (dev-id)
      (with-memory-blocks ((a 'float n)
                           (b 'float n)
                           (c 'float n))
        (random-init a n)
        (random-init b n)
        (sync-memory-block a :host-to-device)
        (sync-memory-block b :host-to-device)
        (vec-add-kernel a b c n
                        :grid-dim (list blocks-per-grid 1 1)
                        :block-dim (list threads-per-block 1 1))
        (sync-memory-block c :device-to-host)
        (verify-result a b c n)))))
\end{lstlisting}

\texttt{aref} reads an array element. \texttt{set} writes one.
\texttt{block-dim-x}, \texttt{block-idx-x}, and \texttt{thread-idx-x} are the
kernel language's index variables. The CUDA backend maps them to the CUDA
built-ins.
